      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "High blood pressure (hypertension) is often called the \"silent killer\" because most people do not experience any noticeable warning signs or symptoms, even when blood pressure is dangerously high.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "most people do not experience any noticeable warning signs or symptoms"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Why You Can't Rely on Symptoms"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asymptomatic nature: The vast majority of people with high blood pressure feel completely fine."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The only way to know: You cannot tell if your blood pressure is high without measuring it with a blood pressure cuff.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Potential Signs of Very High Blood Pressure"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "When blood pressure reaches extremely high levels (often referred to as a hypertensive crisis or severe hypertension, typically 180/120 mm Hg or higher), some people may experience symptoms:",
        "reference_indexes": [
          3,
          4,
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Severe, sudden headaches"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Chest pain or pressure"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shortness of breath"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Blurred or double vision"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Dizziness or lightheadedness"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Nosebleeds"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Nausea or vomiting"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heart palpitations (pounding heartbeat)",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3,
              5,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When It Is a Medical Emergency"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If your blood pressure reading is 180/120 mm Hg or higher, wait about 5 minutes and test it again. If it remains that high and you have warning signs like chest pain, shortness of breath, sudden weakness or numbness, difficulty speaking, or vision changes, call 911 immediately .",
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What your recent blood pressure readings have been (if you have checked them)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Whether you are looking for information about preventing or managing high blood pressure"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help share targeted steps or lifestyle guidelines."
      }
